{# The homepage's product card.

   `tema/index.html` gives a homepage card its own, tighter shape than the catalogue's: a fixed
   thumbnail box, the brand as a small uppercase line, one-line name, the price with the struck
   original beside it, and two controls — the yellow add-to-cart and the wishlist heart. The
   catalogue list keeps `components/product_card.html.twig` and its own rules, so this presentation
   is scoped to the homepage's own grid classes and cannot reach a catalogue page.

   What the card shows is still the real record: the real price, the real brand, the real product,
   and the real cart and wishlist posts. #}
<article class="product-card home-product-card">
    {% if product.onSale %}<span class="badge">İndirim</span>{% endif %}
    <a class="product-thumb" href="{{ path('storefront_catalog_product', {slug: product.slug}) }}">
        <img src="{{ product_image_url(product.imagePath) }}" alt="{{ product.imageAlt ?? product.name }}" width="640" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    </a>

    <div class="product-info">
        {% if product.brandName %}<a class="product-brand" href="{{ path('storefront_catalog_brand', {slug: product.brandSlug}) }}">Marka: {{ product.brandName }}</a>{% endif %}
        <a class="product-name" href="{{ path('storefront_catalog_product', {slug: product.slug}) }}">{{ product.name }}</a>
        <div class="product-price">
            {% if product.sellPrice %}{{ product.sellPrice|storefront_money }}{% else %}Fiyat için iletişime geçin{% endif %}
            {%- if product.onSale and product.basePrice %} <span class="product-old">{{ product.basePrice|storefront_money }}</span>{% endif -%}
        </div>
        {# The theme's card puts the yellow add-to-cart and the wishlist heart on one row, and the
           row is sized by that yellow button. A product that cannot be bought today still has to
           keep the row: an empty slot is what makes the cards of a grid sit at different heights,
           and it reads as a card the shop forgot to finish rather than as a product it cannot sell
           right now. So the button is replaced by a disabled control of the same size that says
           why, and the wishlist form is left exactly as it was.

           Nothing here decides what a POST to the cart accepts. The form still carries the CSRF
           token and still posts to the cart route; what it posts is decided there, as before. #}
        <div class="product-actions">
            {% if product.sellPrice is null %}
                <button class="is-disabled" type="button" disabled>Fiyat Sorun</button>
            {% elseif product.sellable %}
                <form class="cart-add-form" method="post" action="{{ path('storefront_cart_add', {id: product.id}) }}">
                    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('cart_add_' ~ product.id) }}">
                    <input type="hidden" name="quantity" value="1">
                    <button class="btn-yellow" type="submit">Sepete Ekle</button>
                </form>
            {% else %}
                <button class="is-disabled" type="button" disabled>Stokta Yok</button>
            {% endif %}
            <form class="wishlist-add-form" method="post" action="{{ path('storefront_wishlist_add', {id: product.id}) }}">
                <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('wishlist_add_' ~ product.id) }}">
                <button class="btn-ghost" type="submit" aria-label="{{ product.name }} ürününü istek listesine ekle">♡</button>
            </form>
        </div>
    </div>
</article>
